#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ba09dd1-d219-5df2-adfa-d88d7b9fc481" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Point or Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "102d79a9-f19c-5735-a3f2-347938820b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-12425" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2abec0420bf322120bb37135af682a7bc04c97c86d7bf1bdfd04012a8355883c.png)\n\nConsider the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ above, and the transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 7, \\qquad y = u + v +1.\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\overline{PQ}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the segment $\\overline{P'Q'}$ in the $xy$-plane. Find $\\overline{P'Q'}.$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+7 \\\\ u+v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overline{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overline{P'Q'}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overline{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n![](images/8c/8c18a13c717349bccc5af673ee81a9cdc56f9f2a5c32ce75dc8e63fe91765b1e.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/16/16c730f4e70bed4d3c8bd9840b457a3cfdad3905b0ada078e71044b73f3f2800.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $\\mathbf T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-2)$ and $(-3,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/61/61508357c514f50b27498bee50dce36d22b7f55f0baf8c2d6a58ad2d53dc879d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "290ea83a-1a47-5260-82b2-346d38e9adcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12426" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd8b1407add93e72afccf2138cc818df63d47fdaa6ccc23e843a5602d8c1dbd8.png)\n\n          \n\nConsider the rectangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: -1\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 1, \\qquad y = u+v\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+1 \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the boundary of $\\Delta$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $\\Delta,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/69/69d92e24d5b0c81e54d3878793efe7c07d16fc963222997fe4fec4ffb3f93bd5.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n![](images/18/1811ded1ce67ec4ebb443816fdf3eb0080b84c6f84852a04b9b66b4e3df35de6.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/11/1110f6582a0a398accdf5a7cda1cd9676e79ecc251fa5ef2c080fc19867e0096.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(1,-2), (3,0), (1,2),$ and $(-1,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/dd/ddd14bdebe21c830396e044721811cf6b9c7bd7db067e4d0ff7d35e02a592894.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that since $\\det(A) = 2 >    0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179430 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/id #uuid "0f180b16-c807-555d-b92c-826a216a31e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12427" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = v - u-6, \\qquad y = v -2u  .\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/13/135271f9b5fd7e9883b49d02ab2abcb2ed268081e887892e5c251c27de1e85de.svg)\n\n- $\\det(A) = 1$\n\n- The transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-u-6 \\\\ v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is ![](images/dc/dc6ad7fdd5273aa2af71efc5963804edb118fee37596c36a6be3bd30b62467cc.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/42/4266dd2d8188293cb54af9bd8431e27b5ec6e5d936f56363fd692b20b0735e74.svg)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\det(A) > 0,$ the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "e6f17219-642b-51d3-bebe-61519ee6651f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12436" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = 3u+4, \\qquad y = 4v - 2u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+4 \\\\ 4v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u \\\\ -2u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is ![](images/78/783a9503543c1305d982dd65a5f3929398f51db97442606bc03f96484aa03e58.svg)\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/75/7582ffa6b92e0cba1d8e303231754cfa98a30cfe242d7a6744442462102a02ed.svg)\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |12| =12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ba09dd1-d219-5df2-adfa-d88d7b9fc481" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Point or Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124ccea5-c558-54e8-931d-ac8f24d5e647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256949} {:answer/id #uuid "91981231-c419-532a-b1a0-f3d52aacc432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(13,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258068} {:answer/id #uuid "9389800b-ab1e-59a9-990e-685c251fb1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258082} {:answer/id #uuid "c439a84e-99e8-5cc9-a96d-e5fe22288db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258499} {:answer/id #uuid "f08db5c5-f719-59c7-88c9-4c17884b91d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124ccea5-c558-54e8-931d-ac8f24d5e647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256949} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cac29fa-2311-5bc0-9f73-f59e7d67297f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fea0aad7-f097-5052-bac7-eb29df7cbe2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124101" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u+v-2,\\,y=u-2v+1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(3,2)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v-2 \\\\ u-2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(9,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ba09dd1-d219-5df2-adfa-d88d7b9fc481" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Point or Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15292884-dc42-57e9-9c54-15240ddfccc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417825} {:answer/id #uuid "73315599-47f7-5acb-b194-b9be8f07b4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-11,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417826} {:answer/id #uuid "79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417828} {:answer/id #uuid "94a311b2-4197-58fb-9f74-f9df05de251a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417829} {:answer/id #uuid "99809079-4b95-5960-860d-5696a69fc28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-11,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417828} :answer-field/id #uuid "69877276-8edf-5389-b9b6-59ddab5b630a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cb153c3-10ae-52e4-a409-49d1a1150376" :question/math-academy-id "q-124153" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+v-1,\\,y=-u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(-3,1)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+v-1 \\\\ -u-3v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+v \\\\ -u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$ \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(-12,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b15a36-4c42-5d07-9a51-dc6ad5c486df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258213} {:answer/id #uuid "ab597bed-1042-5692-b586-e88675ae5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258276} {:answer/id #uuid "c51bd258-ccef-574e-8d8a-369f597425dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258507} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e0901a-36a7-5935-b68d-61058dd5acaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258757} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf750b6-b5ec-59b0-9432-1f0a64b2f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c51bd258-ccef-574e-8d8a-369f597425dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258507} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b527301-31c5-53bf-9a72-f0270d30af58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bc55bfe-e692-5546-9ff3-60dd10f588d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124322" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+3v-5,\\,y=2v+u-4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v-5 \\\\ 2v+u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ae90c1-c064-56e7-a097-a8780cfd4ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257291} {:answer/id #uuid "558d0acd-b95f-508e-818b-d7aed7ccb8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257517} {:answer/id #uuid "68442875-3c8e-5cbe-89fb-8a3004e6152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257705} {:answer/id #uuid "8be768e1-1047-59b7-976d-c2b3c8c6d97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258007} {:answer/id #uuid "f87afd55-6808-569a-9937-dbc59aca01bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68442875-3c8e-5cbe-89fb-8a3004e6152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257705} :answer-field/id #uuid "f465cacf-0fa4-5950-9818-e0e076a19708" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76a65107-91d8-5259-9f73-6e2fee8f6ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124330" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=-2v+1,\\,y=4u-v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-4$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v+1 \\\\ 4u-v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63f7b66f-541e-5b32-a55d-58357d40b511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01ddff6313a9f86ad9cb750a23f65900f7031afef2f30826efecba379064f3f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257659} {:answer/id #uuid "70273f91-72a5-50f2-ab1a-08b812dd115d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c297c5a3c483cacf056d9765bca2e31d8056c157d334c2f1571e775cf671e579.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257766} {:answer/id #uuid "87aa79cd-0149-5f08-922c-5f0324280937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/4762d50ba86ed040fa1021afdedde9a7d23c873b2a8f5f434752f6e69af2bbb7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257969} {:answer/id #uuid "8d03fdd2-b966-5e4e-8cfc-6b898e809139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/93b061334bdf3b55345bd8e42bf9003f75d9f9221cd8b45d738f3c2bb1523d3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258022} {:answer/id #uuid "baf6ae7b-f1bf-598e-a793-42d965e68dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/741501ec388180aca96136e286f1e871eeaa4e89c3ad36558fd5a384e6951001.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baf6ae7b-f1bf-598e-a793-42d965e68dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/741501ec388180aca96136e286f1e871eeaa4e89c3ad36558fd5a384e6951001.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258421} :answer-field/id #uuid "f795437e-9943-5a42-b8f8-60ebc080c6fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d4da39e-0907-5e10-8ce6-cd308b5a0917" :question/math-academy-id "q-124558" :question/problem "![](images/43/43eb119ef43e136682d4451e4f85f007c63568f7a34880c46f87ab45122f5dd0.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=2-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-u \\\\ 2-v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/a8/a8a13c579c3ca8cc32f997c48e237c38d449aa07040de658589fa5cc68a02c55.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(2,2),(-1,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/14/146f9f36deeac0f3a7108a173485eb6d0d03138d18998a96dc9c3566bee21ca2.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=1>0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2379fc8c-e9f0-5b35-9623-37108593cb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01ddff6313a9f86ad9cb750a23f65900f7031afef2f30826efecba379064f3f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371709} {:answer/id #uuid "9680956f-d146-5b40-aabe-deb395c80e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c1219b3ee5d914ab1f202d337fe16ef1dc19ac654ddf8e214b01b9d58fb3633d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371710} {:answer/id #uuid "75147f28-7ace-5c77-adfe-15cd88e009a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/93b061334bdf3b55345bd8e42bf9003f75d9f9221cd8b45d738f3c2bb1523d3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371711} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c23112-ac00-51d7-8933-86eb45d7a811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3c550025c6f5e7d5227f3ec1ce3ddf9a5efb20531217fdcb9bed5605e7c36e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371712} {:answer/id #uuid "c635eb69-7db6-538d-a8a8-b30e79d8eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/9392484eb99e17956e9a91c7d68b671d1d9e08a87804a86552c18c188cfdeb64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c23112-ac00-51d7-8933-86eb45d7a811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3c550025c6f5e7d5227f3ec1ce3ddf9a5efb20531217fdcb9bed5605e7c36e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371712} :answer-field/id #uuid "662f580a-3722-5158-b5a5-982aabf13cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d30c881c-58fe-5ecb-bbb8-134fe86398ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-124634" :question/problem "![](images/be/be30ef880c451fec095190537577b09896aa6b849e99b90f8185501ee0ef1296.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u-v-1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u-v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/06/06ceacb05656e99e545561e5cd5733529a1cf7f052beb6086b2fd42667041713.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,-2),(1,0),(2,-1),$ and $(0,-3).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/92/92b9079151925202a5346e6b1dd2082b21a4e4102720673773e958c5f3644c4f.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-2<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e9a443-79c4-5055-8bf5-e828053d84d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371995} {:answer/id #uuid "3172d89a-2dcc-560d-a62a-a221fb530105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371996} {:answer/id #uuid "9af5ab04-abbb-5e0d-b9e1-126b263417a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371997} {:answer/id #uuid "128276b9-8ed8-5cf9-b12a-9060590e5d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371998} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb0e067-3a51-5735-a6e9-204ac8516a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e9a443-79c4-5055-8bf5-e828053d84d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371995} :answer-field/id #uuid "423586d1-8fd5-5b16-a1d1-51c2288808b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b737dc54-d234-56ac-a9cc-ffa80f3129e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162142" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|2|=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc4fab2-f286-5943-a45f-a20492b01ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417824} {:answer/id #uuid "76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417827} {:answer/id #uuid "ca24dbee-3bf2-5067-b768-9753b1ef80ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417831} {:answer/id #uuid "d2847d2c-57b6-59e8-b9ce-cd6f7d5c8fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417832} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b0cc75-4660-5f89-b375-205d3cc92d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417827} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6bf158-bc5a-5b83-aecb-e172b7a9b66e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "914af94d-7042-54e6-acb1-fed694c9a4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-162181" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$"}]}
